# What must never reach GitHub, and why.
#
# Read this before adding an exception. The repository is about to be pushed
# to a hosting account, and two of the entries below are not tidiness - they
# are the difference between a private project and a disclosure.

# --- her business, not ours -------------------------------------------------
# migrated/ is the real workbook: 579 named shops, 286 contact names, their
# phone numbers, and 13 months of who ordered what. It belongs to the
# wholesaler. A private repo is one settings click and one added collaborator
# away from not being private, and a git history keeps a file long after it is
# deleted. The deployed app never needs these: the import runs from the
# machine that already holds them, straight into the hosted database.
migrated/

# --- secrets ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The JWT signing key and the database password. Anyone holding the JWT secret
# can mint a token for any account, including an admin one.
server/.env
web/.env
*.env.local
!server/.env.example

# --- big, generated, or specific to one machine -----------------------------
node_modules/

# The portable Node and MariaDB, the database files, and the logs. About
# 130 MB, and GitHub refuses a single file over 100 MB anyway.
runtime/

# Built by the Dockerfile during deploy, and shipped to Windows inside the
# offline zip. Committing it means a stale bundle can be served.
web/dist/
deskops-offline-deps.zip

# --- test and tooling output ------------------------------------------------
tests/node_modules/
tests/test-results/
tests/playwright-report/
tests/shot*.mjs
*.log

# --- editors and operating systems ------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.vscode/
.idea/

# --- this assistant's working folder ----------------------------------------
Claude outputs/
